       1	# Priority Queue Candidate Contract v1
       2	
       3	This document is the normative interface between the evaluator and an untrusted
       4	bounded priority queue implementation.
       5	
       6	## Required artifact
       7	
       8	Candidates provide `priority-queue-candidate.so` in the workspace root and
       9	export the C ABI declared by `include/vibesys_priority_queue_abi.h`. The header
      10	is authoritative for symbols, signatures, statuses, and ABI version.
      11	
      12	The evaluator loads the library directly into an evaluator-owned Rust process.
      13	The candidate does not implement a service or communicate with the Go
      14	correctness checker.
      15	
      16	## Lifecycle
      17	
      18	`vspq_abi_version` returns `VSPQ_ABI_VERSION`. The runner calls
      19	`vspq_queue_create` with an item capacity, maximum value size, and exact
      20	producer and consumer counts. Capacity is measured in items.
      21	
      22	The runner creates one handle per producer and consumer before use. Each handle
      23	is confined to one native thread. It destroys all handles before destroying the
      24	queue and drains the queue before normal benchmark destruction.
      25	
      26	## Operations
      27	
      28	`vspq_try_enqueue` receives an unsigned 64-bit priority and a borrowed byte
      29	slice:
      30	
      31	- `VSPQ_OK` means the priority and a copy of the complete value were retained.
      32	- `VSPQ_FULL` means the item capacity was occupied and nothing was retained.
      33	- The input pointer is valid only during the call.
      34	- Length must not exceed the configured maximum value size.
      35	
      36	`vspq_try_dequeue` receives caller-owned output storage:
      37	
      38	- `VSPQ_OK` copies and removes the queued value with the lowest numeric
      39	  priority and sets `output_length`.
      40	- Equal-priority values may be returned in any order.
